Z||000000||stimpy||05-08-2001||02:42 PM||||I bought $40 worth of headlight motors to rebuld, unfortuntely the gears are still sh!t. I guess I need to pony up and buy some new gears, but how in heck do I take the old gear off of the shaft? AAAARRGH! Otherwise they are in nice shape, the "smooth knob" remanufactured style, bumpers in perfect shape and all.  BTW, these are early style motors.<P>Help me Mr. Wizard!||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||05-08-2001||02:52 PM||||Please take this for what it's worth.  I'm working from memory here...<P>I think removing the nylon gear from the shaft involved pressing the shaft out, using a deep socket and chucking a punch in a drill press, using the drill press as a makeshift arbor press.  Alternative: backup the gear with a socket, and use a punch and hammer.<P>I realize it's easier to say this than to do it, but if you're replacing the gear anyway, you won't mind breaking the gear.  And BTW, I don't think I've ever had to replace a gear, just cleaned the hardened grease out from between its teeth, using a toothpick.  Flossing is optional   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Don't forget to try the trick of removing the output crank and using the other 180 degrees of gear, too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>CORRECTION:  Forget what I said in 1st paragraph.  Now my memory tells me that to remove the gear, you open up the jaws of a vise, to receive the "butterfly" on the shaft.  Set the gear on top of the jaws of the vise, "butterfly" end down.  Put a spare nut partway onto the threads on the upper end of the shaft (to protect the threads), then pound on shaft (spare nut, actually) with hammer.<P>Good grief, this forgetfulness gets worse every year.  Hmmm.... did I take my Alzheimer's medication today  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ?     <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||stimpy||05-08-2001||03:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>I don't think I've ever had to replace a gear, just cleaned the hardened grease out from between its teeth, using a toothpick.  Flossing is optional    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Don't forget to try the trick of removing the output crank and using the other 180 degrees of gear, too    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought I heard about folks doing that. How do I know which way to put the gear in though? I gotta say I didn't really pay attention to theangle of the dangle when I took the gear out.  If the motor is sitting upright, what o'clock should I position the flat spot?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||05-08-2001||11:30 PM||||Well now that thats over. I never said go buy the $35 special socket. I use the cheapest junk sockets I can find. But nevermind.||||reg||1
