Z||000005||TOM||05-08-2001||11:34 PM||||ok lets try this again. When the motor is together and installed there is only two way the shaft with the gear can go in so the the arm that drives the light is in the right place. Do you have a together one to look at. Then just put it the same way. If its wrong then turn it 180. Sometimes things are alot easier to do then to type how to do them. Hope this helps. If not just buy me flush kit.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||05-08-2001||11:37 PM||||I didn't forget you, stimpy.  Back home (Wichita), I've got a sketch somewhere that shows how to reassemble the headlight linkage.  Actually, most of the time I just do one motor at a time, and use the other one in the car as a guide.<P>Anyway, the gist of the "use the other half of the gear" method is:  You take off the nut that attaches the output crank to the shaft, take the crank off and put it back on 180 degrees from the way you found it.  And then put the nut back on, of course.<P>You can do this because the shaft has 2 flats, and the crank has a double-d hole in it.  When you rotate the crank back to where it's supposed to be, and put the motor back together, you'll be using the other (unworn) half of the gear.  Hope this made <B>some</B> sense.<P>If you don't have another motor or a sketch of how to put the linkage together, you can puzzle it out.  Just expect to do it more than once  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">       ||||reg||9
